 132.2, "end": 137.26, "text": " and reveal the structural forces that actually dictate system survival.", "tokens": [51413, 293, 10658, 264, 15067, 5874, 300, 767, 36071, 1185, 12559, 13, 51666], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.058817942117907335, "compression_ratio": 1.654275092936803, "no_speech_prob": 1.0983503603151368e-12}, {"id": 26, "seek": 13726, "start": 137.26, "end": 142.62, "text": " But to do that, we kind of have to start by completely dismantling our basic vocabulary.", "tokens": [50365, 583, 281, 360, 300, 11, 321, 733, 295, 362, 281, 722, 538, 2584, 30506, 1688, 527, 3875, 19864, 13, 50633], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07999427318572998, "compression_ratio": 1.6830065359477124, "no_speech_prob": 1.4155199365081805e-12}, {"id": 27, "seek": 13726, "start": 143.28, "end": 147.7, "text": " Right. Because according to this essay, maintenance and repair are just not what you think they are.", "tokens": [50666, 1779, 13, 1436, 4650, 281, 341, 16238, 11, 11258, 293, 10535, 366, 445, 406, 437, 291, 519, 436, 366, 13, 50887], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07999427318572998, "compression_ratio": 1.6830065359477124, "no_speech_prob": 1.4155199365081805e-12}, {"id": 28, "seek": 13726, "start": 147.92, "end": 153.04, "text": " No, not at all. It's a really common misconception to treat them as points on the same spectrum. Like", "tokens": [50898, 883, 11, 406, 412, 439, 13, 467, 311, 257, 534, 2689, 41350, 281, 2387, 552, 382, 2793, 322, 264, 912, 11143, 13, 1743, 51154], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07999427318572998, "compression_ratio": 1.6830065359477124, "no_speech_prob": 1.4155199365081805e-12}, {"id": 29, "seek": 13726, "start": 153.04, "end": 158.29999999999998, "text": " usually people think of maintenance and repair as the exact same activity, just separated by scale.", "tokens": [51154, 2673, 561, 519, 295, 11258, 293, 10535, 382, 264, 1900, 912, 5191, 11, 445, 12005, 538, 4373, 13, 51417], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07999427318572998, "compression_ratio": 1.6830065359477124, "no_speech_prob": 1.4155199365081805e-12}, {"id": 30, "seek": 13726, "start": 158.45999999999998, "end": 163.23999999999998, "text": " Yeah, like brushing your teeth is a tiny repair and getting a root canal is a massive repair. Same", "tokens": [51425, 865, 11, 411, 33130, 428, 7798, 307, 257, 5870, 10535, 293, 1242, 257, 5593, 9911, 307, 257, 5994, 10535, 13, 10635, 51664], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07999427318572998, "compression_ratio": 1.6830065359477124, "no_speech_prob": 1.4155199365081805e-12}, {"id": 31, "seek": 13726, "start": 163.23999999999998, "end": 164.42, "text": " concept, different size.", "tokens": [51664, 3410, 11, 819, 2744, 13, 51723], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07999427318572998, "compression_ratio": 1.6830065359477124, "no_speech_prob": 1.4155199365081805e-12}, {"id": 32, "seek": 16442, "start": 164.42, "end": 170.82, "text": " That's the intuition, sure. But the text aggressively pushes back on that framing. It", "tokens": [50365, 663, 311, 264, 24002, 11, 988, 13, 583, 264, 2487, 32024, 21020, 646, 322, 300, 28971, 13, 467, 50685], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12069440149998927, "compression_ratio": 1.67578125, "no_speech_prob": 1.2989584451464364e-12}, {"id": 33, "seek": 16442, "start": 170.82, "end": 175.27999999999997, "text": " argues that treating them as different sizes of the same thing conflates operationally distinct", "tokens": [50685, 38218, 300, 15083, 552, 382, 819, 11602, 295, 264, 912, 551, 1497, 75, 1024, 6916, 379, 10644, 50908], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12069440149998927, "compression_ratio": 1.67578125, "no_speech_prob": 1.2989584451464364e-12}, {"id": 34, "seek": 16442, "start": 175.27999999999997, "end": 181.7, "text": " mechanisms. The author insists that maintenance, which the text denotes mathematically as M sub T", "tokens": [50908, 15902, 13, 440, 3793, 50137, 300, 11258, 11, 597, 264, 2487, 1441, 17251, 44003, 382, 376, 1422, 314, 51229], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12069440149998927, "compression_ratio": 1.67578125, "no_speech_prob": 1.2989584451464364e-12}, {"id": 35, "seek": 16442, "start": 181.7, "end": 187.67999999999998, "text": " and repair denoted as rho sub T, are fundamentally different kinds of interventions. And the difference", "tokens": [51229, 293, 10535, 1441, 23325, 382, 20293, 1422, 314, 11, 366, 17879, 819, 3685, 295, 20924, 13, 400, 264, 2649, 51528], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12069440149998927, "compression_ratio": 1.67578125, "no_speech_prob": 1.2989584451464364e-12}, {"id": 36, "seek": 16442, "start": 187.67999999999998, "end": 189.76, "text": " lies entirely in their triggering conditions.", "tokens": [51528, 9134, 7696, 294, 641, 40406, 4487, 13, 51632], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12069440149998927, "compression_ratio": 1.67578125, "no_speech_prob": 1.2989584451464364e-12}, {"id": 37, "seek": 18976, "start": 189.76, "end": 193.98, "text": " Let's break down those triggering conditions then, because I need to get my head around this.", "tokens": [50365, 961, 311, 1821, 760, 729, 40406, 4487, 550, 11, 570, 286, 643, 281, 483, 452, 1378, 926, 341, 13, 50576], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1758384132385254, "compression_ratio": 1.6349809885931559, "no_speech_prob": 1.567555980852564e-12}, {"id": 38, "seek": 18976, "start": 194.7, "end": 197.28, "text": " How does the paper define maintenance?", "tokens": [50612, 1012, 775, 264, 3035, 6964, 11258, 30, 50741], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1758384132385254, "compression_ratio": 1.6349809885931559, "no_speech_prob": 1.567555980852564e-12}, {"id": 39, "seek": 18976, "start": 198.07999999999998, "end": 200.84, "text": " So maintenance is defined as unconditional.", "tokens": [50781, 407, 11258, 307, 7642, 382, 47916, 13, 50919], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1758384132385254, "compression_ratio": 1.6349809885931559, "no_speech_prob": 1.567555980852564e-12}, {"id": 40, "seek": 18976, "start": 201.6, "end": 202.16, "text": " Unconditional.", "tokens": [50957, 1156, 18882, 2628, 13, 50985], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1758384132385254, "compression_ratio": 1.6349809885931559, "no_speech_prob": 1.567555980852564e-12}, {"id": 41, "seek": 18976, "start": 202.28, "end": 208.88, "text": " Right. It's an intervention applied at every single time step continuously to reduce deviation before it", "tokens": [50991, 1779, 13, 467, 311, 364, 13176, 6456, 412, 633, 2167, 565, 1823, 15684, 281, 5407, 25163, 949, 309, 51321], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1758384132385254, "compression_ratio": 1.6349809885931559, "no_speech_prob": 1.567555980852564e-12}, {"id": 42, "seek": 18976, "start": 208.88, "end": 214.22, "text": " even has a chance to compound. You execute maintenance regardless of whether anything is visibly", "tokens": [51321, 754, 575, 257, 2931, 281, 14154, 13, 509, 14483, 11258, 10060, 295, 1968, 1340, 307, 1452, 3545, 51588], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1758384132385254, "compression_ratio": 1.6349809885931559, "no_speech_prob": 1.567555980852564e-12}, {"id": 43, "seek": 18976, "start": 214.22, "end": 214.62, "text": " broken.", "tokens": [51588, 5463, 13, 51608], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1758384132385254, "compression_ratio": 1.6349809885931559, "no_speech_prob": 1.567555980852564e-12}, {"id": 44, "seek": 18976, "start": 214.94, "end": 216.1, "text": " You don't wait for a signal.", "tokens": [51624, 509, 500, 380, 1699, 337, 257, 6358, 13, 51682], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1758384132385254, "compression_ratio": 1.6349809885931559, "no_speech_prob": 1.567555980852564e-12}, {"id": 45, "seek": 21610, "start": 216.1, "end": 218.94, "text": " Exactly. You do not wait for a signal of failure.", "tokens": [50365, 7587, 13, 509, 360, 406, 1699, 337, 257, 6358, 295, 7763, 13, 50507], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12933912484542184, "compression_ratio": 1.6844444444444444, "no_speech_prob": 1.6298388420127319e-12}, {"id": 46, "seek": 21610, "start": 219.2, "end": 223.1, "text": " Okay, so the toothbrushing analogy actually works for the unconditional part. You don't", "tokens": [50520, 1033, 11, 370, 264, 11680, 1443, 5371, 21663, 767, 1985, 337, 264, 47916, 644, 13, 509, 500, 380, 50715], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12933912484542184, "compression_ratio": 1.6844444444444444, "no_speech_prob": 1.6298388420127319e-12}, {"id": 47, "seek": 21610, "start": 223.1, "end": 228.44, "text": " wait for your teeth to hurt. You just brush them unconditionally every day as like a continuous", "tokens": [50715, 1699, 337, 428, 7798, 281, 4607, 13, 509, 445, 5287, 552, 34959, 15899, 633, 786, 382, 411, 257, 10957, 50982], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12933912484542184, "compression_ratio": 1.6844444444444444, "no_speech_prob": 1.6298388420127319e-12}, {"id": 48, "seek": 21610, "start": 228.44, "end": 229.54, "text": " baseline operation.", "tokens": [50982, 20518, 6916, 13, 51037], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12933912484542184, "compression_ratio": 1.6844444444444444, "no_speech_prob": 1.6298388420127319e-12}, {"id": 49, "seek": 21610, "start": 229.92, "end": 234.48, "text": " The toothbrushing captures the continuous rhythm. Yeah, definitely. But let's look at repair.", "tokens": [51056, 440, 11680, 1443, 5371, 27986, 264, 10957, 11801, 13, 865, 11, 2138, 13, 583, 718, 311, 574, 412, 10535, 13, 51284], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12933912484542184, "compression_ratio": 1.6844444444444444, "no_speech_prob": 1.6298388420127319e-12}, {"id": 50, "seek": 21610, "start": 234.76, "end": 236.5, "text": " Repair is strictly conditional.", "tokens": [51298, 3696, 1246, 307, 20792, 27708, 13, 51385], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12933912484542184, "compression_ratio": 1.6844444444444444, "no_speech_prob": 1.6298388420127319e-12}, {"id": 51, "seek": 23650, "start": 236.5, "end": 241.84, "text": " In the mathematical language of the text, repair is an intervention that is triggered only after", "tokens": [50365, 682, 264, 18894, 2856, 295, 264, 2487, 11, 10535, 307, 364, 13176, 300, 307, 21710, 787, 934, 50632], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11009768752364425, "compression_ratio": 1.6714801444043321, "no_speech_prob": 1.8318018542989867e-12}, {"id": 52, "seek": 23650, "start": 241.84, "end": 244.84, "text": " the system has been ejected from the viability manifold.", "tokens": [50632, 264, 1185, 575, 668, 32520, 292, 490, 264, 1932, 2310, 47138, 13, 50782], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11009768752364425, "compression_ratio": 1.6714801444043321, "no_speech_prob": 1.8318018542989867e-12}, {"id": 53, "seek": 23650, "start": 245.2, "end": 250.04, "text": " The viability manifold. That sounds like a hyperdrive component on a sci-fi spaceship.", "tokens": [50800, 440, 1932, 2310, 47138, 13, 663, 3263, 411, 257, 9848, 45524, 6542, 322, 257, 2180, 12, 13325, 39185, 13, 51042], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11009768752364425, "compression_ratio": 1.6714801444043321, "no_speech_prob": 1.8318018542989867e-12}, {"id": 54, "seek": 23650, "start": 250.44, "end": 253.02, "text": " I assume it means something a bit more grounded in systems theory.", "tokens": [51062, 286, 6552, 309, 1355, 746, 257, 857, 544, 23535, 294, 3652, 5261, 13, 51191], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11009768752364425, "compression_ratio": 1.6714801444043321, "no_speech_prob": 1.8318018542989867e-12}, {"id": 55, "seek": 23650, "start": 253.3, "end": 259.02, "text": " It's a highly specific geometric term, yeah. But conceptually, you can just think of the viability", "tokens": [51205, 467, 311, 257, 5405, 2685, 33246, 1433, 11, 1338, 13, 583, 3410, 671, 11, 291, 393, 445, 519, 295, 264, 1932, 2310, 51491], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11009768752364425, "compression_ratio": 1.6714801444043321, "no_speech_prob": 1.8318018542989867e-12}, {"id": 56, "seek": 23650, "start": 259.02, "end": 262.64, "text": " manifold as the safe operating zone of a system.", "tokens": [51491, 47138, 382, 264, 3273, 7447, 6668, 295, 257, 1185, 13, 51672], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11009768752364425, "compression_ratio": 1.6714801444043321, "no_speech_prob": 1.8318018542989867e-12}, {"id": 57, "seek": 23650, "start": 262.78, "end": 263.0, "text": " Gotcha.", "tokens": [51679, 42109, 13, 51690], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11009768752364425, "compression_ratio": 1.6714801444043321, "no_speech_prob": 1.8318018542989867e-12}, {"id": 58, "seek": 26300, "start": 263.0, "end": 268.86, "text": " Gotcha. So when a system is inside the viability manifold, things are functioning within acceptable", "tokens": [50365, 42109, 13, 407, 562, 257, 1185, 307, 1854, 264, 1932, 2310, 47138, 11, 721, 366, 18483, 1951, 15513, 50658], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06366590733798046, "compression_ratio": 1.685512367491166, "no_speech_prob": 1.361297901660008e-12}, {"id": 59, "seek": 26300, "start": 268.86, "end": 274.34, "text": " parameters. The system is viable. Repair only happens when a threshold is crossed and the", "tokens": [50658, 9834, 13, 440, 1185, 307, 22024, 13, 3696, 1246, 787, 2314, 562, 257, 14678, 307, 14622, 293, 264, 50932], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06366590733798046, "compression_ratio": 1.685512367491166, "no_speech_prob": 1.361297901660008e-12}, {"id": 60, "seek": 26300, "start": 274.34, "end": 276.84, "text": " system is violently ejected from that safe zone.", "tokens": [50932, 1185, 307, 46728, 32520, 292, 490, 300, 3273, 6668, 13, 51057], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06366590733798046, "compression_ratio": 1.685512367491166, "no_speech_prob": 1.361297901660008e-12}, {"id": 61, "seek": 26300, "start": 276.92, "end": 279.36, "text": " When something is actively demonstrably broken.", "tokens": [51061, 1133, 746, 307, 13022, 5516, 5305, 356, 5463, 13, 51183], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06366590733798046, "compression_ratio": 1.685512367491166, "no_speech_prob": 1.361297901660008e-12}, {"id": 62, "seek": 26300, "start": 279.56, "end": 285.82, "text": " Correct. And to understand why a rational system would ever choose to delay a fix, you know,", "tokens": [51193, 12753, 13, 400, 281, 1223, 983, 257, 15090, 1185, 576, 1562, 2826, 281, 8577, 257, 3191, 11, 291, 458, 11, 51506], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06366590733798046, "compression_ratio": 1.685512367491166, "no_speech_prob": 1.361297901660008e-12}, {"id": 63, "seek": 26300, "start": 285.82, "end": 290.72, "text": " why it would choose to risk falling out of that manifold, the author introduces four foundational", "tokens": [51506, 983, 309, 576, 2826, 281, 3148, 7440, 484, 295, 300, 47138, 11, 264, 3793, 31472, 1451, 32195, 51751], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06366590733798046, "compression_ratio": 1.685512367491166, "no_speech_prob": 1.361297901660008e-12}, {"id": 64, "seek": 29072, "start": 290.72, "end": 295.16, "text": " variables. And we need to spend some time on these, right? Because they form the engine of the entire", "tokens": [50365, 9102, 13, 400, 321, 643, 281, 3496, 512, 565, 322, 613, 11, 558, 30, 1436, 436, 1254, 264, 2848, 295, 264, 2302, 50587], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11998331321860259, "compression_ratio": 1.6244897959183673, "no_speech_prob": 2.1166195966065837e-12}, {"id": 65, "seek": 29072, "start": 295.16, "end": 299.16, "text": " theorem. They absolutely do. And the first two variables are all about overhead.", "tokens": [50587, 20904, 13, 814, 3122, 360, 13, 400, 264, 700, 732, 9102, 366, 439, 466, 19922, 13, 50787], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11998331321860259, "compression_ratio": 1.6244897959183673, "no_speech_prob": 2.1166195966065837e-12}, {"id": 66, "seek": 29072, "start": 299.38000000000005, "end": 304.74, "text": " Overhead? Meaning, just to clarify, not the cost of the actual fix itself, but the cost of the", "tokens": [50798, 4886, 1934, 30, 19948, 11, 445, 281, 17594, 11, 406, 264, 2063, 295, 264, 3539, 3191, 2564, 11, 457, 264, 2063, 295, 264, 51066], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11998331321860259, "compression_ratio": 1.6244897959183673, "no_speech_prob": 2.1166195966065837e-12}, {"id": 67, "seek": 29072, "start": 304.74, "end": 308.36, "text": " capacity to fix. Precisely. That's a great distinction. Let's start with the first one,", "tokens": [51066, 6042, 281, 3191, 13, 48746, 736, 13, 663, 311, 257, 869, 16844, 13, 961, 311, 722, 365, 264, 700, 472, 11, 51247], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11998331321860259, "compression_ratio": 1.6244897959183673, "no_speech_prob": 2.1166195966065837e-12}, {"id": 68, "seek": 29072, "start": 308.42, "end": 310.58000000000004, "text": " which is kappa M. Okay, kappa M.", "tokens": [51250, 597, 307, 350, 25637, 376, 13, 1033, 11, 350, 25637, 376, 13, 51358], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11998331321860259, "compression_ratio": 1.6244897959183673, "no_speech_prob": 2.1166195966065837e-12}, {"id": 69, "seek": 31058, "start": 310.58, "end": 316.08, "text": " This is the fixed overhead of continuous maintenance. This encompasses the cost of monitoring the system,", "tokens": [50365, 639, 307, 264, 6806, 19922, 295, 10957, 11258, 13, 639, 49866, 264, 2063, 295, 11028, 264, 1185, 11, 50640], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09195546022395498, "compression_ratio": 1.6472727272727272, "no_speech_prob": 1.5485667219025667e-12}, {"id": 70, "seek": 31058, "start": 316.28, "end": 321.59999999999997, "text": " running inspections, maintaining a standing infrastructure of readiness. You pay kappa M", "tokens": [50650, 2614, 46544, 11, 14916, 257, 4877, 6896, 295, 34954, 13, 509, 1689, 350, 25637, 376, 50916], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09195546022395498, "compression_ratio": 1.6472727272727272, "no_speech_prob": 1.5485667219025667e-12}, {"id": 71, "seek": 31058, "start": 321.59999999999997, "end": 325.06, "text": " unconditionally, whether things are broken or perfectly fine.", "tokens": [50916, 34959, 15899, 11, 1968, 721, 366, 5463, 420, 6239, 2489, 13, 51089], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09195546022395498, "compression_ratio": 1.6472727272727272, "no_speech_prob": 1.5485667219025667e-12}, {"id": 72, "seek": 31058, "start": 325.06, "end": 329.09999999999997, "text": " Let me try to map this to something tangible, just to be sure I'm separating the cost of the", "tokens": [51089, 961, 385, 853, 281, 4471, 341, 281, 746, 27094, 11, 445, 281, 312, 988, 286, 478, 29279, 264, 2063, 295, 264, 51291], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09195546022395498, "compression_ratio": 1.6472727272727272, "no_speech_prob": 1.5485667219025667e-12}, {"id": 73, "seek": 31058, "start": 329.09999999999997, "end": 335.88, "text": " intervention from the overhead. So kappa M is like deciding to pay for a premium monthly gym membership", "tokens": [51291, 13176, 490, 264, 19922, 13, 407, 350, 25637, 376, 307, 411, 17990, 281, 1689, 337, 257, 12049, 12878, 9222, 16560, 51630], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09195546022395498, "compression_ratio": 1.6472727272727272, "no_speech_prob": 1.5485667219025667e-12}, {"id": 74, "seek": 33588, "start": 335.88, "end": 341.34, "text": " and putting a personal trainer and a nutritionist on retainer. Right. You're paying that fixed cost", "tokens": [50365, 293, 3372, 257, 2973, 21110, 293, 257, 14718, 468, 322, 18340, 260, 13, 1779, 13, 509, 434, 6229, 300, 6806, 2063, 50638], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07928923404578006, "compression_ratio": 1.7250755287009063, "no_speech_prob": 1.1922509420697613e-12}, {"id": 75, "seek": 33588, "start": 341.34, "end": 346.4, "text": " every single month unconditionally just to monitor your health and avoid having a heart attack.", "tokens": [50638, 633, 2167, 1618, 34959, 15899, 445, 281, 6002, 428, 1585, 293, 5042, 1419, 257, 1917, 2690, 13, 50891], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07928923404578006, "compression_ratio": 1.7250755287009063, "no_speech_prob": 1.1922509420697613e-12}, {"id": 76, "seek": 33588, "start": 346.65999999999997, "end": 350.84, "text": " That perfectly captures the essence of standing infrastructure. You're paying for the readiness", "tokens": [50904, 663, 6239, 27986, 264, 12801, 295, 4877, 6896, 13, 509, 434, 6229, 337, 264, 34954, 51113], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07928923404578006, "compression_ratio": 1.7250755287009063, "no_speech_prob": 1.1922509420697613e-12}, {"id": 77, "seek": 33588, "start": 350.84, "end": 355.32, "text": " and the continuous monitoring. Okay, making sense so far. Now, opposed to that, we have the second", "tokens": [51113, 293, 264, 10957, 11028, 13, 1033, 11, 1455, 2020, 370, 1400, 13, 823, 11, 8851, 281, 300, 11, 321, 362, 264, 1150, 51337], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07928923404578006, "compression_ratio": 1.7250755287009063, "no_speech_prob": 1.1922509420697613e-12}, {"id": 78, "seek": 33588, "start": 355.32, "end": 361.65999999999997, "text": " variable, which is kappa R. This is the fixed overhead of initiating a single discontinuous repair", "tokens": [51337, 7006, 11, 597, 307, 350, 25637, 497, 13, 639, 307, 264, 6806, 19922, 295, 6265, 990, 257, 2167, 31420, 12549, 10535, 51654], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07928923404578006, "compression_ratio": 1.7250755287009063, "no_speech_prob": 1.1922509420697613e-12}, {"id": 79, "seek": 33588, "start": 361.65999999999997, "end": 365.86, "text": " event. Discontinuous meaning a one-off shock. Exactly. But again, this is not the", "tokens": [51654, 2280, 13, 4208, 9000, 259, 12549, 3620, 257, 472, 12, 4506, 5588, 13, 7587, 13, 583, 797, 11, 341, 307, 406, 264, 51864], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07928923404578006, "compression_ratio": 1.7250755287009063, "no_speech_prob": 1.1922509420697613e-12}, {"id": 80, "seek": 36586, "start": 365.86, "end": 371.32, "text": " cost of the actual medical procedure or the parts. It's the administrative and organizational", "tokens": [50365, 2063, 295, 264, 3539, 4625, 10747, 420, 264, 3166, 13, 467, 311, 264, 17900, 293, 24730, 50638], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0556512491418681, "compression_ratio": 1.7326007326007327, "no_speech_prob": 2.513501993345324e-12}, {"id": 81, "seek": 36586, "start": 371.32, "end": 378.40000000000003, "text": " overhead of just initiating the crisis response. So following that health analogy, kappa R isn't", "tokens": [50638, 19922, 295, 445, 6265, 990, 264, 5869, 4134, 13, 407, 3480, 300, 1585, 21663, 11, 350, 25637, 497, 1943, 380, 50992], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0556512491418681, "compression_ratio": 1.7326007326007327, "no_speech_prob": 2.513501993345324e-12}, {"id": 82, "seek": 36586, "start": 378.40000000000003, "end": 383.98, "text": " the cost of the open heart surgery. It's the flat administrative fee of being admitted to the", "tokens": [50992, 264, 2063, 295, 264, 1269, 1917, 7930, 13, 467, 311, 264, 4962, 17900, 12054, 295, 885, 14920, 281, 264, 51271], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0556512491418681, "compression_ratio": 1.7326007326007327, "no_speech_prob": 2.513501993345324e-12}, {"id": 83, "seek": 36586, "start": 383.98, "end": 389.08000000000004, "text": " emergency room. Yes. It's the cost of walking through the hospital doors, tying up the triage", "tokens": [51271, 7473, 1808, 13, 1079, 13, 467, 311, 264, 2063, 295, 4494, 807, 264, 4530, 8077, 11, 32405, 493, 264, 1376, 609, 51526], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0556512491418681, "compression_ratio": 1.7326007326007327, "no_speech_prob": 2.513501993345324e-12}, {"id": 84, "seek": 36586, "start": 389.08000000000004, "end": 394.36, "text": " nurses, getting a bed all before a doctor even looks at your heart or gives you a single pill.", "tokens": [51526, 17446, 11, 1242, 257, 2901, 439, 949, 257, 4631, 754, 1542, 412, 428, 1917, 420, 2709, 291, 257, 2167, 8100, 13, 51790], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0556512491418681, "compression_ratio": 1.7326007326007327, "no_speech_prob": 2.513501993345324e-12}, {"id": 85, "seek": 39436, "start": 394.36, "end": 399.24, "text": " That is a very useful way to distinguish them. And this distinction is exactly why the essay", "tokens": [50365, 663, 307, 257, 588, 4420, 636, 281, 20206, 552, 13, 400, 341, 16844, 307, 2293, 983, 264, 16238, 50609], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09853086973491468, "compression_ratio": 1.5772058823529411, "no_speech_prob": 1.3938760085388524e-12}, {"id": 86, "seek": 39436, "start": 399.24, "end": 403.6, "text": " insists that previous models failed. Oh, really? How did they fail?", "tokens": [50609, 50137, 300, 3894, 5245, 7612, 13, 876, 11, 534, 30, 1012, 630, 436, 3061, 30, 50827], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09853086973491468, "compression_ratio": 1.5772058823529411, "no_speech_prob": 1.3938760085388524e-12}, {"id": 87, "seek": 39436, "start": 404.12, "end": 409.78000000000003, "text": " Well, earlier models treated repair capacity as one big undifferentiated bucket of organizational", "tokens": [50853, 1042, 11, 3071, 5245, 8668, 10535, 6042, 382, 472, 955, 674, 15790, 72, 770, 13058, 295, 24730, 51136], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09853086973491468, "compression_ratio": 1.5772058823529411, "no_speech_prob": 1.3938760085388524e-12}, {"id": 88, "seek": 39436, "start": 409.78000000000003, "end": 415.02000000000004, "text": " effort. But Flixian's paper separates them because, you know, it takes a very specific,", "tokens": [51136, 4630, 13, 583, 3235, 970, 952, 311, 3035, 34149, 552, 570, 11, 291, 458, 11, 309, 2516, 257, 588, 2685, 11, 51398], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09853086973491468, "compression_ratio": 1.5772058823529411, "no_speech_prob": 1.3938760085388524e-12}, {"id": 89, "seek": 39436, "start": 415.36, "end": 420.08000000000004, "text": " continuous kind of organizational energy to maintain a standing monitoring system.", "tokens": [51415, 10957, 733, 295, 24730, 2281, 281, 6909, 257, 4877, 11028, 1185, 13, 51651], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09853086973491468, "compression_ratio": 1.5772058823529411, "no_speech_prob": 1.3938760085388524e-12}, {"id": 90, "seek": 42008, "start": 420.08, "end": 424.71999999999997, "text": " Your kappa M. Right. And it takes a completely different shock-based kind of organizational", "tokens": [50365, 2260, 350, 25637, 376, 13, 1779, 13, 400, 309, 2516, 257, 2584, 819, 5588, 12, 6032, 733, 295, 24730, 50597], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06897146436903212, "compression_ratio": 1.5946745562130178, "no_speech_prob": 1.68754994787218e-12}, {"id": 91, "seek": 42008, "start": 424.71999999999997, "end": 430.0, "text": " energy to trigger a crisis response. That's your kappa R. So you're paying for the bureaucracy", "tokens": [50597, 2281, 281, 7875, 257, 5869, 4134, 13, 663, 311, 428, 350, 25637, 497, 13, 407, 291, 434, 6229, 337, 264, 44671, 50861], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06897146436903212, "compression_ratio": 1.5946745562130178, "no_speech_prob": 1.68754994787218e-12}, {"id": 92, "seek": 42008, "start": 430.0, "end": 434.8, "text": " of the fix, not just the fix itself. Yeah. That makes so much sense. Okay. So we have our", "tokens": [50861, 295, 264, 3191, 11, 406, 445, 264, 3191, 2564, 13, 865, 13, 663, 1669, 370, 709, 2020, 13, 1033, 13, 407, 321, 362, 527, 51101], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06897146436903212, "compression_ratio": 1.5946745562130178, "no_speech_prob": 1.68754994787218e-12}, {"id": 93, "seek": 42008, "start": 434.8, "end": 437.76, "text": " two overheads. What are the other two variables that complete this picture?", "tokens": [51101, 732, 19922, 82, 13, 708, 366, 264, 661, 732, 9102, 300, 3566, 341, 3036, 30, 51249], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06897146436903212, "compression_ratio": 1.5946745562130178, "no_speech_prob": 1.68754994787218e-12}, {"id": 94, "seek": 42008, "start": 438.02, "end": 442.02, "text": " The next one is arguably the most insidious force in any system, and that's delta.", "tokens": [51262, 440, 958, 472, 307, 26771, 264, 881, 1028, 327, 851, 3464, 294, 604, 1185, 11, 293, 300, 311, 8289, 13, 51462], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06897146436903212, "compression_ratio": 1.5946745562130178, "no_speech_prob": 1.68754994787218e-12}, {"id": 95, "seek": 42008, "start": 442.44, "end": 449.12, "text": " Delta? Yeah. Delta represents the delay amplification rate. It measures how fast an uncorrected problem", "tokens": [51483, 18183, 30, 865, 13, 18183, 8855, 264, 8577, 9731, 3774, 3314, 13, 467, 8000, 577, 2370, 364, 6219, 284, 2554, 292, 1154, 51817], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06897146436903212, "compression_ratio": 1.5946745562130178, "no_speech_prob": 1.68754994787218e-12}, {"id": 96, "seek": 44912, "start": 449.12, "end": 454.64, "text": " compounds between time steps. Oh. If you notice a defect and you decide to do absolutely nothing,", "tokens": [50365, 21810, 1296, 565, 4439, 13, 876, 13, 759, 291, 3449, 257, 16445, 293, 291, 4536, 281, 360, 3122, 1825, 11, 50641], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11246714632735293, "compression_ratio": 1.6013513513513513, "no_speech_prob": 1.371970245744869e-12}, {"id": 97, "seek": 44912, "start": 455.2, "end": 460.72, "text": " delta dictates how much worse that specific defect gets by tomorrow. Compounding deviation.", "tokens": [50669, 8289, 12569, 1024, 577, 709, 5324, 300, 2685, 16445, 2170, 538, 4153, 13, 6620, 24625, 25163, 13, 50945], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11246714632735293, "compression_ratio": 1.6013513513513513, "no_speech_prob": 1.371970245744869e-12}, {"id": 98, "seek": 44912, "start": 461.36, "end": 466.38, "text": " The silent killer. Yeah. So if the crack in my windshield grows by a fraction of a millimeter", "tokens": [50977, 440, 12784, 13364, 13, 865, 13, 407, 498, 264, 6226, 294, 452, 39996, 13156, 538, 257, 14135, 295, 257, 17942, 51228], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11246714632735293, "compression_ratio": 1.6013513513513513, "no_speech_prob": 1.371970245744869e-12}, {"id": 99, "seek": 44912, "start": 466.38, "end": 472.88, "text": " every time I hit a pothole, that's delta in action. Yes, exactly. And finally, we have pay T,", "tokens": [51228, 633, 565, 286, 2045, 257, 280, 900, 4812, 11, 300, 311, 8289, 294, 3069, 13, 1079, 11, 2293, 13, 400, 2721, 11, 321, 362, 1689, 314, 11, 51553], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11246714632735293, "compression_ratio": 1.6013513513513513, "no_speech_prob": 1.371970245744869e-12}, {"id": 100, "seek": 44912, "start": 473.1, "end": 478.1, "text": " which represents the convexity of the correction cost. Convexity. Right. This answers a slightly", "tokens": [51564, 597, 8855, 264, 42432, 507, 295, 264, 19984, 2063, 13, 2656, 303, 87, 507, 13, 1779, 13, 639, 6338, 257, 4748, 51814], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11246714632735293, "compression_ratio": 1.6013513513513513, "no_speech_prob": 1.371970245744869e-12}, {"id": 101, "seek": 47810, "start": 478.1, "end": 482.58000000000004, "text": " different question. As the physical problem grows, how much more expensive does it get to", "tokens": [50365, 819, 1168, 13, 1018, 264, 4001, 1154, 13156, 11, 577, 709, 544, 5124, 775, 309, 483, 281, 50589], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05382167282751051, "compression_ratio": 1.6645569620253164, "no_speech_prob": 1.0441317949139162e-12}, {"id": 102, "seek": 47810, "start": 482.58000000000004, "end": 486.94, "text": " fundamentally correct it? Because in real life, fixing a problem that has grown twice as large", "tokens": [50589, 17879, 3006, 309, 30, 1436, 294, 957, 993, 11, 19442, 257, 1154, 300, 575, 7709, 6091, 382, 2416, 50807], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05382167282751051, "compression_ratio": 1.6645569620253164, "no_speech_prob": 1.0441317949139162e-12}, {"id": 103, "seek": 47810, "start": 486.94, "end": 490.12, "text": " is rarely just twice as expensive. Right. It isn't linear.", "tokens": [50807, 307, 13752, 445, 6091, 382, 5124, 13, 1779, 13, 467, 1943, 380, 8213, 13, 50966], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05382167282751051, "compression_ratio": 1.6645569620253164, "no_speech_prob": 1.0441317949139162e-12}, {"id": 104, "seek": 47810, "start": 490.48, "end": 495.56, "text": " Far from it. It's often exponentially more expensive. And that exponential upward curve", "tokens": [50984, 9067, 490, 309, 13, 467, 311, 2049, 37330, 544, 5124, 13, 400, 300, 21510, 23452, 7605, 51238], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05382167282751051, "compression_ratio": 1.6645569620253164, "no_speech_prob": 1.0441317949139162e-12}, {"id": 105, "seek": 47810, "start": 495.56, "end": 500.8, "text": " is governed by the parameter p. You know, before we move on to how these four variables actually", "tokens": [51238, 307, 35529, 538, 264, 13075, 280, 13, 509, 458, 11, 949, 321, 1286, 322, 281, 577, 613, 1451, 9102, 767, 51500], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05382167282751051, "compression_ratio": 1.6645569620253164, "no_speech_prob": 1.0441317949139162e-12}, {"id": 106, "seek": 47810, "start": 500.8, "end": 505.16, "text": " interact to create the theorem, I want to stop and look at something specific the text points out", "tokens": [51500, 4648, 281, 1884, 264, 20904, 11, 286, 528, 281, 1590, 293, 574, 412, 746, 2685, 264, 2487, 2793, 484, 51718], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05382167282751051, "compression_ratio": 1.6645569620253164, "no_speech_prob": 1.0441317949139162e-12}, {"id": 107, "seek": 50516, "start": 505.16, "end": 509.72, "text": " about that compounding rate, delta. The author goes out of their way to establish a baseline", "tokens": [50365, 466, 300, 14154, 278, 3314, 11, 8289, 13, 440, 3793, 1709, 484, 295, 641, 636, 281, 8327, 257, 20518, 50593], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08418213555572229, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.6809495418865228e-12}, {"id": 108, "seek": 50516, "start": 509.72, "end": 515.64, "text": " where delta equals zero. And they say this isn't just some degenerate special case we should ignore.", "tokens": [50593, 689, 8289, 6915, 4018, 13, 400, 436, 584, 341, 1943, 380, 445, 512, 40520, 473, 2121, 1389, 321, 820, 11200, 13, 50889], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08418213555572229, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.6809495418865228e-12}, {"id": 109, "seek": 50516, "start": 516.32, "end": 522.4, "text": " Why is it so crucial to understand a world where delta is zero before we introduce the compounding", "tokens": [50923, 1545, 307, 309, 370, 11462, 281, 1223, 257, 1002, 689, 8289, 307, 4018, 949, 321, 5366, 264, 14154, 278, 51227], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08418213555572229, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.6809495418865228e-12}, {"id": 110, "seek": 50516, "start": 522.4, "end": 528.44, "text": " chaos? It's a critical theoretical baseline because delta equals zero represents a scenario where", "tokens": [51227, 14158, 30, 467, 311, 257, 4924, 20864, 20518, 570, 8289, 6915, 4018, 8855, 257, 9005, 689, 51529], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08418213555572229, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.6809495418865228e-12}, {"id": 111, "seek": 50516, "start": 528.44, "end": 534.8000000000001, "text": " deviation accumulates, but it doesn't amplify itself endogenously. Endogenously, meaning from within.", "tokens": [51529, 25163, 12989, 26192, 11, 457, 309, 1177, 380, 41174, 2564, 917, 8799, 5098, 13, 6967, 8799, 5098, 11, 3620, 490, 1951, 13, 51847], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08418213555572229, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.6809495418865228e-12}, {"id": 112, "seek": 53480, "start": 534.8, "end": 541.4, "text": " Exactly. Think of a backlog of unrelated small defects. A pile of paperwork on a desk is a really", "tokens": [50365, 7587, 13, 6557, 295, 257, 47364, 295, 38967, 1359, 32655, 13, 316, 14375, 295, 27953, 322, 257, 10026, 307, 257, 534, 50695], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.031967247354573215, "compression_ratio": 1.7247386759581882, "no_speech_prob": 1.524887204354397e-12}, {"id": 113, "seek": 53480, "start": 541.4, "end": 546.3199999999999, "text": " great mental image for this. I have plenty of those. We all do. But if you ignore the paperwork,", "tokens": [50695, 869, 4973, 3256, 337, 341, 13, 286, 362, 7140, 295, 729, 13, 492, 439, 360, 13, 583, 498, 291, 11200, 264, 27953, 11, 50941], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.031967247354573215, "compression_ratio": 1.7247386759581882, "no_speech_prob": 1.524887204354397e-12}, {"id": 114, "seek": 53480, "start": 546.4599999999999, "end": 550.8399999999999, "text": " the pile definitely gets taller as new papers arrive. But the papers sitting at the bottom of", "tokens": [50948, 264, 14375, 2138, 2170, 22406, 382, 777, 10577, 8881, 13, 583, 264, 10577, 3798, 412, 264, 2767, 295, 51167], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.031967247354573215, "compression_ratio": 1.7247386759581882, "no_speech_prob": 1.524887204354397e-12}, {"id": 115, "seek": 53480, "start": 550.8399999999999, "end": 555.7199999999999, "text": " the pile don't magically interact with each other to spawn new papers. A pile of papers that doesn't", "tokens": [51167, 264, 14375, 500, 380, 39763, 4648, 365, 1184, 661, 281, 17088, 777, 10577, 13, 316, 14375, 295, 10577, 300, 1177, 380, 51411], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.031967247354573215, "compression_ratio": 1.7247386759581882, "no_speech_prob": 1.524887204354397e-12}, {"id": 116, "seek": 53480, "start": 555.7199999999999, "end": 561.3399999999999, "text": " breathe. Exactly. The problem grows linearly based on external inputs, not geometrically based on its own", "tokens": [51411, 10192, 13, 7587, 13, 440, 1154, 13156, 43586, 2361, 322, 8320, 15743, 11, 406, 12956, 81, 984, 2361, 322, 1080, 1065, 5