  L55: But if we apply the lens of the five theoretical essays we're diving into today, which were written in 2026 by an author known only as Flixion, that polished state is an absolute illusion.
  L59: A total illusion. The overarching thesis of this entire Flixion corpus is that these systems are not made of static states at all.
  L107: But Fliction looks at complex systems and just completely rejects that. For human minds, for legal frameworks, for software architecture, states are not the ground floor.
  L111: Not at all. In these papers, specifically the ones titled Against Eventless Ontology in History Before Function, Fliction argues that states are actually just equivalence classes of compressed trajectories.
  L255: We traded visibility for speed. And this brings us to what Flickshen calls the anti-alignment theorem. And honestly, it is one of the most sobering concepts in these papers.
  L711: Fletian outlines three very specific mechanisms to cure mastery saturation without abandoning the environment.
  L931: Fliction argues that unstructured time is not the absence of a solution to boredom.
  L1023: Fliction argues that constraints, whether it's a local zoning law, a corporate HR policy, or a safety protocol on an oil rig, they are not static walls.
  L1371: Fliction focuses heavily on the compounding rate.
  L1455: But the crucial, unforgiving caveat in Fliction's math is that this deferral window is always finite.
  L1735: An implication Fliction's math points directly toward, but that we haven't explicitly named yet.
